<sn><md>**Environmental Settings:**

- These soils develop under `sub-humid to humid` and `cryic to thermic` climatic environments. 
- They occupy approximately `6.9%` of the world’s land area and occur most commonly in the temperate grasslands of the middle latitudes. Typically Mollisols are formed `under grassland vegetation` (include both short-grass steppe and tall grass prairie). 
- They are extensive in `North America, South America, Asia and Europe`.
</md></sn><sn><md>**Pedogenic processes:**

- `Melanization`, the process of darkening of the soil by addition and decomposition of OM is the dominant process in Mollisols. 
- It is the process by which `mollic epipedon forms and dark soil` colors extend down into the profile. 
- Melanization and the accompanying accumulation of OC are very dominant in temperate grassland soils. 
- `Translocation` of clays i.e. `Lessivage` may occur in some Mollisols but the expression of this process is extremely variable. 
- `Cumulization` or the addition of mineral materials to the soil surface can be an important process in many Mollisols.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Properties:

* Mollisols are the `best soils`. The structure of the soils is `very strong`.
* They are characterized by `deep, dark`, relatively `fertile` surface horizon known as `mollic epipedon`.
* Have `high water holding capacity`.
* Have `high pH` and rich in $\text{CaCO}_3$.
* Presence of dominant `smectite minerals` that has `high CEC`.
* Have BSP $&gt;50\%$.
* Basic `cations are high`.
* Have `good nutrient status`.
* Soils are `highly productive` and have good `chemical fertility`.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Uses:

* Mollisols are inherently the `best agricultural soils` and `produce optimum yields` both under irrigated and non-irrigated conditions with optimum inputs.
* In the `rainfed Mediterranean regions` such soils are used for `wheat cultivation`.
* In the `drier regions` these soils are used for `grazing`.
* In the sub-humid regions they are used for `cultivation of wheat, maize and a variety of vegetable crops`.
* Under irrigated conditions they are `intensively cultivated` for `growing plantation crops`.</md></sn>